WN.chunk(37,{"n":[[1366,"Make things move by pushing and pulling (a swing, a wagon, a door) and notice that a bigger push makes things go further or faster",["Shows a push and a pull with a toy","Says that a harder push made the ball roll further","Uses push and pull to describe opening a door or drawer"],[]],[1367,"Roll balls and cars down ramps, see that things roll faster on a steeper slope and that round things roll while flat things slide or stop",["Builds a ramp with a board and books","Finds that a steeper ramp makes the car go faster","Sorts objects into ones that roll and ones that do not"],[]],[1368,"Name and describe forces using precise vocabulary — force, push, pull, twist, stretch, squash, contact force, non-contact force, gravity, weight, friction, air resistance, upthrust — and distinguish between forces that require physical contact and forces that act at a distance",["Correctly name the force acting in at least five given scenarios (e.g. 'friction slows the sledge', 'gravity pulls the apple')","Distinguish between contact forces (friction, upthrust) and non-contact forces (gravity, magnetism) using the correct terms","Use 'weight' correctly as a force — distinct from 'mass' — in descriptions"],[]],[1369,"Understand that pushes and pulls are forces that can change the speed or direction of an object's motion, and compare the effects of different strengths and directions",["Describe a push and a pull as types of force that make objects move, speed up, slow down, or change direction","Compare the effects of a gentle push versus a strong push on the same object","Predict the direction an object will move based on the direction of the applied force"],["ngss-k5:K-PS2-1"]],[1370,"Notice that some forces need contact between two objects (contact forces) while magnetic forces can act at a distance (non-contact forces)",["Define contact forces as those needing objects to touch (e.g. push, pull, friction)","Define non-contact forces as those acting at a distance (e.g. magnetism)","Sort examples of forces into contact and non-contact categories"],["uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.2"]],[1371,"Draw and interpret force diagrams showing forces as labelled arrows — where the arrow's length represents the force's magnitude and its direction shows which way the force acts; show multiple forces on one object; identify from the diagram whether forces are balanced (equal arrows in opposite directions, no resultant) or unbalanced (arrows of different sizes, producing a resultant); represent the resultant with a single arrow",["Draw a force diagram with labelled arrows showing direction and relative size for at least two forces acting on an object","Use their diagram to explain whether forces are balanced or unbalanced and what will happen to the object","Add a resultant force arrow to a diagram and explain how they calculated it"],[]],[1372,"Compare how things move on different surfaces, noticing that some surfaces create more friction than others",["Observe and compare how the same object moves on at least three different surfaces","Describe that rough surfaces slow objects down more than smooth surfaces","Use the word 'friction' to explain why movement differs on different surfaces"],["uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.1"]],[1373,"Observe how magnets attract or repel each other and attract some materials and not others",["Demonstrate that magnets attract some materials (iron, steel, nickel) but not others (wood, plastic, copper)","Show that two magnets can either attract (pull together) or repel (push apart)","Test at least six objects and correctly predict which are magnetic"],["uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.3","uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.4"]],[1374,"Describe magnets as having two poles (north and south) and predict whether two magnets will attract or repel based on which poles face each other",["Identify and label the north and south poles of a magnet","State the rule: like poles repel, opposite poles attract","Predict the outcome of bringing two magnets together based on their pole orientation"],["ngss-k5:3-PS2-3","ngss-k5:3-PS2-4","uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.5","uk-nc-2013:Y3.Sci.FM.6"]],[1375,"Plan and conduct an investigation to provide evidence of the effects of balanced and unbalanced forces on the motion of an object",["Define balanced forces (equal in size, opposite in direction — no change in motion) and unbalanced forces (cause motion to change)","Plan a fair test investigating how different force sizes affect an object's motion","Use results as evidence to explain when forces are balanced versus unbalanced"],["ngss-k5:3-PS2-1"]],[1376,"Identify the effects of air resistance, water resistance, and friction, and understand that these forces act between moving surfaces to oppose motion",["Define air resistance, water resistance, and friction as forces that oppose motion","Give everyday examples of each force and explain how they slow things down","Describe how these forces depend on factors like speed, surface area, or surface texture"],["uk-nc-2013:Y5.Sci.F.2"]],[1377,"Use technical vocabulary for force and motion — balanced forces, unbalanced forces, resultant force, acceleration, deceleration, speed, moment, lever, fulcrum, mechanical advantage — and apply these when explaining and predicting how forces affect the motion and position of objects",["Distinguish between balanced and unbalanced forces and describe the effect of each on an object's motion","Use 'resultant force' correctly when describing the net effect of two or more forces acting on an object","Apply 'moment', 'lever', and 'fulcrum' correctly when describing how simple machines work"],[]],[1378,"Explain that unsupported objects fall towards the Earth because of the force of gravity acting between the Earth and the falling object",["Define gravity as a force of attraction between the Earth and objects","Explain that unsupported objects fall because gravity pulls them towards the Earth","Give examples showing gravity in action (dropping objects, jumping, water flowing downhill)"],["ngss-k5:5-PS2-1","uk-nc-2013:Y5.Sci.F.1"]],[1379,"Recognise that some mechanisms including levers, pulleys, and gears allow a smaller force to have a greater effect",["Describe how a lever works: effort at one end moves a load at the other, with a pivot in between","Explain how a pulley changes the direction of a force and can make lifting easier","Describe how gears transfer and change the size or direction of forces"],["uk-nc-2013:Y5.Sci.F.3"]],[1380,"Read and plot distance-time graphs for moving objects; interpret the gradient (steepness) of a line as speed; identify stationary periods (horizontal sections), constant speed (straight diagonal lines), and relative speeds by comparing gradients; calculate average speed from the gradient of a straight-line segment using speed = distance ÷ time",["Read a distance-time graph and describe what is happening at each stage — moving, stopped, returning","Calculate speed from the gradient of a straight section of a distance-time graph","Sketch a distance-time graph from a written description of a journey with stops and speed changes"],[]],[1381,"Describe magnetic poles (north and south), explain attraction and repulsion between poles, describe magnetic field lines plotted using a compass, and explain the Earth's magnetic field and its practical uses",["States the rule for attraction and repulsion of magnetic poles","Draws the magnetic field pattern around a bar magnet from memory or compass readings","Explains why a compass needle points north","Explains that the Earth has a magnetic field and describes how it is used for navigation"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-5","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.8"]],[1382,"Distinguish between mass (amount of matter, measured in kg) and weight (gravitational force, measured in N), use the equation weight = mass × gravitational field strength, and explain why g differs on other planets and stars",["Explains the difference between mass and weight with correct units for each","Calculates weight using W = mg with g = 10 N/kg on Earth","Predicts what would happen to an object's weight on the Moon or Jupiter","Explains why mass stays constant but weight changes in different gravitational fields"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-4","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.10"]],[1383,"Describe forces as vector quantities with both magnitude and direction, distinguish between balanced forces (zero resultant, no change in motion) and unbalanced forces (non-zero resultant, causes acceleration or deceleration)",["Explains what a vector quantity is and why force is a vector","Calculates the resultant force when two forces act in the same or opposite directions on an object","Explains what happens to an object's motion when forces are balanced vs unbalanced","Draws free body diagrams showing all forces on a stationary object (e.g. a book on a table)"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-1","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.4"]],[1384,"Calculate average speed using the equation speed = distance ÷ time, represent journeys on distance-time graphs, and interpret gradient as speed and flat sections as stationary periods",["Uses speed = distance ÷ time to calculate average speed with correct units (m/s, km/h)","Draws a distance-time graph for a given journey with correct axes and labels","Reads a distance-time graph to determine speed, stopping points, and direction of travel","Identifies which section of a distance-time graph represents the fastest speed"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-2","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.1","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.2"]],[1385,"Explain forces associated with deforming objects (elastic and inelastic deformation), thermal expansion and contraction of materials, and how fluid pressure acts in all directions and increases with depth",["Distinguishes elastic deformation (returns to shape) from inelastic/plastic deformation (permanently changed)","Explains why bridges and railway tracks have expansion gaps","Explains why pressure increases with depth in a liquid (e.g. why deep-sea divers need pressure suits)","Gives an everyday example of thermal expansion causing a problem or being used usefully"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.5"]],[1386,"Describe the magnetic effect of an electric current (a current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field), and investigate how the strength of an electromagnet depends on current, number of coil turns, and core material",["Describes that a current-carrying wire produces a circular magnetic field","Lists three factors that affect electromagnet strength: current size, number of coil turns, and core material","Explains why an electromagnet can be switched on and off, unlike a permanent magnet"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-3"]],[1387,"Calculate the turning effect (moment = force × perpendicular distance), explain how pressure is transmitted equally in liquids (Pascal's principle) and the concept of atmospheric pressure, and describe Hooke's Law (extension ∝ force up to the elastic limit)",["Calculates the moment of a force and uses the principle of moments to solve lever problems","Explains why hydraulic systems can multiply force (pressure transmitted equally)","States Hooke's Law and plots force-extension graphs identifying the elastic limit","Calculates spring constant k from a force-extension graph (k = F/x)"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-2","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.6","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.9"]],[1388,"State and apply Newton's First Law (an object stays at rest or constant velocity unless acted on by a resultant force) and Second Law (force = mass × acceleration), including the relationship between mass, force, and acceleration",["States Newton's First Law and gives a real example (e.g. why a moving spacecraft doesn't need engines in space)","Uses F = ma to calculate force, mass, or acceleration given the other two quantities","Explains why a heavier object requires more force to achieve the same acceleration","Explains why seatbelts are needed in cars using Newton's First Law"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-1","ngss-ms:MS-PS2-2","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.7"]],[1389,"State and apply Newton's Third Law: every force has an equal and opposite reaction force acting on a different object, distinguishing action-reaction pairs from balanced forces",["States Newton's Third Law correctly, identifying both the action and reaction force and the objects they act on","Gives at least two real-world examples (e.g. rocket propulsion, swimmer pushing off a wall)","Distinguishes a Newton's Third Law pair from balanced forces on the same object","Explains why a gun recoils when fired using Newton's Third Law"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-1","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.7"]],[1390,"Calculate the net (resultant) force on an object in one dimension by adding forces with signs for direction, and predict whether the object speeds up, slows down or keeps a constant velocity",["Calculates the resultant of opposing forces","Uses positive and negative signs for direction","Predicts the motion from the net force"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-2"]],[1391,"Draw free-body diagrams for a single object showing every force acting on it as a labelled arrow from the object, with lengths showing relative size, and use them to find the resultant force",["Draws all forces on a single object (weight, normal, friction, tension, drag)","Labels each force with its type","Uses arrow lengths to show balanced or unbalanced forces"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Sci.Phys.MotionAndForces.4"]],[1392,"Explain inertia as an object's tendency to resist changes in its motion, with mass as its measure, and use it to explain seatbelts, headrests and why heavy objects are hard to start and stop",["Defines inertia","Explains why a passenger lurches forward when a bus brakes","Explains why more massive objects need bigger forces to change motion"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-2"]],[1393,"Explain the motor effect as the force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field, and describe how this principle is used in electric motors and loudspeakers",["Explains that a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field experiences a force (the motor effect)","Predicts how reversing the current or reversing the magnetic field affects the direction of the force","Describes how the motor effect is applied in an electric motor and in a loudspeaker"],["ngss-ms:MS-PS2-5"]],[1394,"Distinguish scalar quantities (magnitude only: distance, speed, mass, energy) from vector quantities (magnitude and direction: displacement, velocity, force, acceleration), and represent vectors as arrows",["Classifies quantities as scalar or vector","Explains the difference between distance and displacement","Draws vectors to scale with arrows"],["ccss-math:N-VM.1"]],[1395,"Define acceleration as the rate of change of velocity (a = Δv/t, in m/s²), including deceleration as negative acceleration, and calculate it from data",["Calculates acceleration from change in velocity and time","Explains what 2 m/s² means in words","Interprets negative acceleration"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1396,"Use F = ma with the net force to solve multi-step problems — finding acceleration from several forces, or the force needed for a given acceleration — and link to the equations of motion",["Calculates acceleration from net force and mass","Finds the driving force needed against friction for a given acceleration","Combines F = ma with a = Δv/t"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1397,"Explain that pressure in a liquid increases with depth and density (p = ρgh), and use it to explain dams, diving and upthrust",["Calculates pressure at a given depth","Explains why dams are thicker at the bottom","Explains upthrust as a pressure difference"],[]],[1398,"Use displacement and velocity as signed quantities in one dimension, choosing a positive direction, and calculate average velocity as displacement ÷ time",["Chooses a positive direction and uses negative values for the opposite way","Calculates average velocity for a journey that turns back","Explains how velocity can change while speed stays constant"],[]],[1399,"Investigate Hooke's law (F = ke), plot force–extension graphs, find the spring constant from the gradient and identify the limit of proportionality",["Plots force against extension","Calculates the spring constant from the gradient","Identifies where the graph stops being linear"],[]],[1400,"Explain that without air resistance all objects near Earth's surface fall with the same acceleration (g ≈ 9.8 m/s²), regardless of mass, and calculate speeds of falling objects",["States that a hammer and feather fall together in a vacuum","Explains why heavier objects don't fall faster, using F = ma","Calculates the speed of a falling object after a given time"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1401,"Define momentum as mass × velocity (p = mv, in kg m/s), recognise it as a vector, and compare the momentum of different objects",["Calculates momentum with correct units","Explains why a slow truck can have more momentum than a fast bike","Uses sign to show direction of momentum"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-2"]],[1402,"Explain stopping distance as thinking distance plus braking distance, and how speed, reaction time, road conditions and vehicle condition affect each",["Distinguishes thinking and braking distance","Lists factors affecting each","Explains why braking distance increases more than speed"],[]],[1403,"Draw and interpret velocity–time graphs, using the gradient to find acceleration and the area under the graph to find displacement",["Identifies constant velocity, acceleration and deceleration sections","Calculates acceleration from the gradient","Calculates distance travelled from the area under the graph"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1404,"Add perpendicular vectors using Pythagoras' theorem and trigonometry, and resolve a force or velocity into horizontal and vertical components",["Finds the resultant of two perpendicular forces","Resolves a force at an angle into components","Solves a boat-crossing-a-river velocity problem"],["ccss-math:N-VM.3","ccss-math:N-VM.4a"]],[1405,"Use conservation of momentum (total momentum before = after when no external force acts) to calculate velocities after collisions in one dimension",["States the conservation of momentum principle","Calculates the velocity after two objects stick together","Explains conservation using Newton's third law"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-2","ngss-ms:MS-PS2-1"]],[1406,"Design, evaluate and refine a device that minimises the force on an object during a collision (e.g. egg-drop protector, helmet, crumple zone), using impulse reasoning and test data",["Uses impulse ideas to justify design choices","Tests designs and records results","Refines the design based on evidence"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-3"]],[1407,"Use the equations of motion for constant acceleration (v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², v² = u² + 2as, s = ½(u + v)t), choosing the right one and rearranging to find unknowns",["Lists known and unknown quantities before choosing an equation","Solves a problem using v² = u² + 2as","Checks the answer for sign and size"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1408,"Explain non-contact forces using fields — gravitational, electric and magnetic — drawing field-line diagrams and describing field strength and direction",["Draws field lines around a magnet, a charge and a planet","Explains what closer field lines mean","Explains how a field transfers a force without contact"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-4","ngss-hs:HS-PS3-5"]],[1409,"Model friction as depending on the normal force and the surfaces, distinguish static friction (up to a maximum) from kinetic friction, and use F ≤ μN in simple problems",["Explains what the normal force is","Distinguishes static and kinetic friction","Calculates maximum friction from μ and N"],[]],[1410,"Relate force to change in momentum (FΔt = Δp, so F = Δp/Δt), and explain why increasing collision time reduces force",["Uses F = Δp/Δt to calculate an average force","Explains why airbags and crumple zones reduce injury","Explains why you bend your knees when landing"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-3"]],[1411,"Find the speed at a particular instant from a curved distance–time graph by drawing a tangent and calculating its gradient, distinguishing it from average speed",["Draws a tangent to a curve at a point","Calculates the gradient of the tangent","Explains the difference between instantaneous and average speed"],[]],[1412,"Map and draw the magnetic field patterns produced by a current in a straight wire (concentric circles) and in a solenoid (like a bar magnet), use the right-hand grip rule to find field direction, and plan an investigation showing that reversing the current reverses the field",["Describes the field pattern around a straight wire","Explains how a solenoid's field is strengthened","Uses the right-hand grip rule to find field direction"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-5"]],[1413,"Analyse data from experiments (e.g. trolleys pulled by different forces) to show that acceleration is proportional to net force and inversely proportional to mass",["Plots acceleration against force and identifies a straight line through the origin","Explains why a vs 1/m gives a straight line","Identifies sources of error in the experiment"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1414,"Apply conservation of momentum to explosions and recoil, where total momentum before is zero, to explain rockets, recoiling guns and jumping off a boat",["Explains why total momentum after an explosion is zero","Calculates a recoil velocity","Explains how a rocket accelerates in space"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-2"]],[1415,"Explain how drag increases with speed until it balances weight so a falling object reaches terminal velocity, and interpret velocity–time graphs for a skydiver",["Describes forces on a skydiver at each stage","Explains why acceleration decreases as drag grows","Interprets the v–t graph of a skydiver opening a parachute"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1416,"Explain that an object moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating towards the centre because its velocity direction changes, requiring a centripetal force supplied by tension, friction or gravity",["Explains why constant speed in a circle still means acceleration","Identifies the force providing the centripetal force in examples","Predicts the path if the centripetal force is removed"],[]],[1417,"Use Coulomb's law F = kq₁q₂/r² to describe and predict the electrostatic force between two charged objects, comparing its form with gravitation",["Calculates the force between two charges","Predicts attraction or repulsion from the signs","Compares the structure of Coulomb's law with gravitation"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-4"]],[1418,"Distinguish elastic collisions (kinetic energy conserved) from inelastic ones (kinetic energy transferred to other stores), noting that momentum is conserved in both",["Checks whether kinetic energy is conserved in a collision","Explains where kinetic energy goes in an inelastic collision","States that momentum is conserved in both types"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-2"]],[1419,"Resolve weight into components parallel and perpendicular to an inclined plane, and calculate the acceleration of an object sliding down a slope with or without friction",["Resolves weight on a slope into two components","Calculates acceleration down a frictionless slope","Includes friction in a slope calculation"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-1"]],[1420,"Use Newton's law of universal gravitation F = Gm₁m₂/r² to describe and predict the gravitational force between two objects, including the inverse-square effect of distance",["Calculates the gravitational force between two masses","Predicts the effect of doubling the distance","Explains why g is smaller on the Moon"],["ngss-hs:HS-PS2-4"]],[1421,"Analyse projectile motion by treating horizontal motion (constant velocity) and vertical motion (constant acceleration g) independently, and calculate range, time of flight and maximum height",["Explains why horizontal and vertical motion are independent","Calculates time of flight for a horizontally launched object","Calculates the range of a projectile"],[]]],"r":[[87,"The magnetic effect of a current requires understanding what an electric current is and how it flows"],[88,"Electromagnetism requires prior understanding of magnetic field lines and field direction — established in the magnetic fields topic"],[126,"Must understand pushes/pulls before investigating balanced vs unbalanced forces"],[127,"Investigating balanced and unbalanced forces requires drawing force diagrams to record and analyse experimental findings"],[128,"Must understand friction affects motion before investigating balanced/unbalanced forces"],[159,"KS3 magnetic field lines and Earth's magnetism extends KS2 magnetic poles, attraction and repulsion"],[211,"The motor effect (force on a current in a field) is an application of electromagnetism — requires knowledge of both magnetic fields and current-produced fields"],[271,"Must know about non-contact forces before investigating magnetic attraction/repulsion"],[314,"KS3 forces as vectors extends KS2 introduction to pushes and pulls changing speed and direction"],[315,"Describing balanced and unbalanced forces as vector quantities requires resultant force, balanced forces vocabulary"],[316,"Forces as vectors with magnitude and direction is the formal underpinning of the force arrow representation"],[317,"KS3 resultant force and balanced forces extends KS2 distinction between contact and non-contact forces"],[351,"Balanced/unbalanced forces supports understanding gravity as an unbalanced force on unsupported objects"],[352,"Explaining gravity requires the force vocabulary: gravity, weight, non-contact force"],[353,"Explaining gravity as a downward force requires representing it as a downward arrow in a force diagram"],[354,"Must know about non-contact forces before learning gravity as a non-contact force"],[365,"Understanding pushes and pulls as forces is supported by the arrow representation of magnitude and direction"],[613,"Newton's Third Law is conventionally taught after the first two — students need Newton's 1st and 2nd as reference points to distinguish reaction pairs from balanced forces"],[632,"Deformation and fluid pressure involve forces acting on objects — the vector force framework gives the conceptual grounding"],[864,"Must observe magnets attract/repel before formalising pole rules"],[952,"Distinguishing contact from non-contact forces requires these exact terms"],[953,"Distinguishing contact and non-contact forces is clarified by drawing force diagrams showing where arrows originate"],[954,"Must experience contact forces like friction before distinguishing contact vs non-contact forces"],[1168,"Interpreting distance–time graphs requires understanding compound units such as metres per second or km/h"],[1224,"Must understand forces change motion before comparing movement on different surfaces"],[1225,"Comparing how things move on different surfaces requires friction vocabulary"],[1235,"Calculating speed as distance ÷ time applies compound-unit thinking from Math"],[1236,"Calculating speed and representing journeys on distance-time graphs requires being able to plot and read these graphs"],[1237,"Must understand forces and their effects before learning how mechanisms multiply forces"],[1238,"Gravity context helps understand why we need mechanisms to lift heavy things"],[1239,"Understanding levers, pulleys, and gears requires moment, lever, and fulcrum vocabulary"],[1405,"KS3 weight = mass × g extends KS2 introduction to gravity as the force pulling objects toward Earth"],[1458,"Newton's 1st and 2nd laws are defined in terms of resultant force — the concept of balanced and unbalanced forces must be understood first"],[1459,"Gravity (weight = mg) is the main worked example for Newton's laws — mass/weight distinction gives concrete numbers for F = ma"],[1460,"Newton's laws explain why friction and air resistance slow objects down — the KS2 observation of resistance now has a theoretical explanation"],[1571,"Moments and pressure are applications of force — understanding forces as vectors and resultant forces provides the foundation"],[1572,"KS3 moments and mechanical advantage extends KS2 introduction to levers, pulleys and gears allowing smaller force for greater effect"],[1710,"Understanding gravity helps contextualise resistive forces opposing gravitational motion"],[1711,"Identifying air resistance, water resistance, and friction requires these force terms"],[1712,"Identifying and explaining air resistance and friction requires drawing force arrows to show opposing forces"],[1713,"Must know friction from surface comparisons before learning about air/water resistance as similar forces"],[2297,"Forces as vectors with magnitude and direction are introduced at KS3"],[2298,"Speed, met with distance–time graphs, is the scalar that velocity extends by adding direction"],[2299,"Displacement and velocity are signed because they are vectors with a direction"],[2300,"Opposite directions are represented by positive and negative numbers"],[2301,"Acceleration is the change in velocity per second, so velocity must be understood as signed"],[2302,"Acceleration is measured in m/s², a compound unit meaning metres per second every second"],[2303,"The gradient of a velocity–time graph is read as the acceleration, Δv/t"],[2304,"Reading distance–time graphs carries over, with the new rule that area gives displacement"],[2305,"Finding acceleration means calculating the gradient of a straight-line section of the graph"],[2306,"Speed as the gradient of a distance–time graph is extended to curves by drawing a tangent"],[2307,"Instantaneous rate is the limit of average rates over shrinking intervals"],[2308,"The four equations hold only when acceleration is constant, so its meaning must be secure"],[2309,"Solving for unknowns requires rearranging formulas with squares"],[2310,"The equations can be derived from the area under a v–t graph"],[2311,"The primary idea that unsupported objects fall is made precise as acceleration g ≈ 9.8 m/s²"],[2312,"Free fall is uniform acceleration, so speeds are found from v = gt"],[2313,"Weight = mg explains why acceleration is independent of mass"],[2314,"Force diagrams are refined into free-body diagrams with every force on one object only"],[2315,"The forces on a free-body diagram are combined to find the resultant force"],[2316,"The net force is found by adding the forces drawn on the free-body diagram"],[2317,"Forces in opposite directions are added using positive and negative numbers"],[2318,"Primary balanced and unbalanced forces are made numerical as a net force of zero or not"],[2319,"Inertia is the idea behind Newton's first law: an object keeps its motion unless a net force acts"],[2320,"The KS3 statement of F = ma is used here in multi-step problems"],[2321,"The F in F = ma is the net force, found by combining all the forces with their signs"],[2322,"The acceleration found from F = ma is linked to changes in velocity by a = Δv/t"],[2323,"Knowing F = ma tells students to look for a ∝ F at fixed mass and a ∝ 1/m at fixed force"],[2324,"A straight line through the origin on a graph of a against F shows proportionality"],[2325,"Acceleration falling as mass rises is inverse proportion, a ∝ 1/m"],[2326,"Terminal velocity changes free fall by adding a drag force that grows with speed"],[2327,"Primary work on air resistance slowing falling objects is the drag force used here"],[2328,"The falling acceleration shrinks because the net force, weight minus drag, shrinks"],[2329,"Friction and the normal force are drawn as arrows on a free-body diagram before calculating"],[2330,"Primary observations that rough surfaces grip more are modelled with a friction coefficient"],[2331,"Momentum is mass times velocity, so it has a direction and can be negative"],[2332,"Newton's laws link momentum to force, since a net force changes an object's motion"],[2333,"Conservation sums the momentum of every object before and after the collision"],[2334,"Equal and opposite third-law forces during a collision explain why momentum is conserved"],[2335,"Primary predictions about what happens when things collide are made quantitative"],[2336,"The unknown velocity after the collision is found by solving a linear equation"],[2337,"Recoil is conservation of momentum with zero total momentum before the explosion"],[2338,"Impulse is defined as the change in momentum, Δp = mv − mu, so momentum must be calculated first"],[2339,"F = Δp/Δt is F = ma rewritten by substituting a = Δv/Δt and multiplying by the mass"],[2340,"Crumple zones and helmets work by increasing the collision time, which lowers the force"],[2341,"Momentum is conserved in both kinds of collision; only kinetic energy separates them"],[2342,"Comparing kinetic energy before and after requires calculating it"],[2343,"Only vector quantities such as force and velocity are added by direction or resolved"],[2344,"Resolving and combining vectors uses right-angled trigonometry"],[2345,"Weight is resolved into components parallel and perpendicular to the slope"],[2346,"The acceleration down the slope comes from the net force along it divided by the mass"],[2347,"On a rough slope, friction acts up the slope and reduces the net force"],[2348,"The vertical motion is constant acceleration g, so it is solved with the suvat equations"],[2349,"The launch velocity is resolved into horizontal and vertical components"],[2350,"Height as a function of time, or of horizontal distance, is a parabola"],[2351,"Constant speed in a circle still means a changing velocity, because its direction changes"],[2352,"Orbits are the familiar example of circular motion under gravity"],[2353,"An acceleration towards the centre needs a net force towards the centre, by F = ma"],[2354,"The KS3 idea that all masses attract is made quantitative with F = Gm₁m₂/r²"],[2355,"Gravity is an inverse-square law, so doubling the distance quarters the force"],[2356,"G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ and planetary masses are handled in scientific notation"],[2357,"Static charge, with like charges repelling and unlike attracting, is the force measured here"],[2358,"Coulomb's law is an inverse-square law, so the force falls with the square of the separation"],[2359,"Comparing F = kq₁q₂/r² with F = Gm₁m₂/r² highlights the shared inverse-square form"],[2360,"KS3 magnetic field lines around a bar magnet are the model for gravitational and electric fields"],[2361,"The primary distinction between contact and non-contact forces is explained using fields"],[2362,"KS3 electromagnets are explained by the field pattern of a current-carrying solenoid"],[2363,"The field around a wire or solenoid is drawn with field lines showing strength and direction"],[2364,"KS3 Hooke's law is investigated with force–extension graphs and a spring constant"],[2365,"F = ke is direct proportion, so the spring constant is the gradient of the straight line"],[2366,"The KS3 idea that liquid pressure increases with depth is made quantitative with p = ρgh"],[2367,"The ρ in p = ρgh is the liquid's density, so denser liquids give greater pressure"],[2368,"Braking distance depends on the deceleration a car can achieve"],[2369,"Braking distance grows with kinetic energy, which grows with speed squared"],[3897,"A ramp gives a toy the push that starts it moving"],[3898,"They guess which toy will roll furthest, then test it"],[4071,"Pushes and pulls are named after children feel them in play"]],"c":{"Science|Forces & Motion|5":"Your child is learning about forces — understanding how pushes and pulls can make objects move faster, slower, or change direction, and testing different ways to affect motion.","Science|Forces & Motion|7":"Your child is exploring forces around them — discovering how magnets work, understanding that some forces need contact while others work at a distance, and investigating how different surfaces affect how things move.","Science|Forces & Motion|8":"Your child is investigating how forces affect movement — observing patterns in how objects move and conducting experiments to understand how balanced and unbalanced forces change an object's motion.","Science|Forces & Motion|9":"Your child is learning about gravity and forces that resist motion like friction and air resistance, while discovering how simple machines like levers and pulleys can make tasks easier.","Science|Forces & Motion|3":"Your child is discovering pushes and pulls — making toys, swings and wagons move, and noticing that a bigger push sends things further.","Science|Forces & Motion|4":"Your child is experimenting with slopes — rolling balls and cars down ramps and finding that steeper slopes are faster and round things roll.","Science|Forces & Motion|13":"Your child is learning to analyse forces — telling scalars from vectors, drawing free-body diagrams, finding the overall force on an object, and understanding inertia.","Science|Forces & Motion|14":"Your child is learning to describe and predict motion — using velocity, acceleration and velocity–time graphs, applying F = ma, and working with momentum, springs, liquid pressure and stopping distances.","Science|Forces & Motion|15":"Your child is learning the laws of motion in depth — using the equations of motion, explaining terminal velocity and friction, conserving momentum in collisions, resolving vectors, and describing fields.","Science|Forces & Motion|16":"Your child is studying advanced mechanics — collisions, forces on slopes, projectile and circular motion, and the inverse-square laws for gravitational and electric forces."}});
